WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.540
YUFENG GUO: Previously
on AI Adventures,

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.610
we looked at Colab
as a great way

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.220
to get started in the data
science and machine learning

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.119
world.

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:10.070
But there are some
models out there

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:13.340
that need to run for a
long time or you want

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.219
to get more memory or GPUs.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:19.700
How can we hook up Colab
with some more compute power?

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.270
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.700
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.130
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.770
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.910
we're going to use Google
Cloud Platform's deep learning

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:47.020
VMs to power your
Colab environment.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.270
A few episodes ago,
we looked at how

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.510
to create deep learning VMs with
your choice of machine learning

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.800
frameworks.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.010
Today, we'll use that VM
and connect Colab to it

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.780
so we can utilize the
resources on that machine.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.019
To start out, we'll of
course need to make our VM.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:07.570
And let's make it a big one.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.970
Head on over to the
Cloud Marketplace,

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.480
and we'll find the deep
learning VM and select it.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.170
OK.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.610
Now, to select our parameters.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.650
Let's give it a name.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.230
Say colab-box.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.780
And for good
measure, we'll do 8--

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.880
no, 16 CPUs, and how
about 60 gigs of memory?

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.420
And now for the GPUs.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.030
Let's choose the V100s.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.500
And we'll have two of them.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.280
Yeah.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.290
Now we're really cooking.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.300
While we're waiting
for that to spin up,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.700
let's talk a bit about
how we're going to get

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.115
Colab to talk to your VM.

00:01:42.115 --> 00:01:43.990
There are two tricks
that we're going to use,

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.240
which together make this
connection possible.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.240
The first is that
Colab can actually

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.250
connect to any local runtime.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.830
So if you have a local Jupyter
Notebook Server running,

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.900
say on your laptop,
you can use Colab

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.870
if you want as your front
end to that server rather

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.450
than the usual Jupyter
Notebook front end.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:06.449
And you can access that in the
upper right-hand menu of Colab.

00:02:06.449 --> 00:02:08.199
The second trick that
we'll take advantage

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:12.130
of is that you can do port
forwarding on the deep learning

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.830
VM we just created so
that it can be accessed

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.610
from your local machine.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.060
You can do that with
the command shown here.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.570
And be sure to run it from
your actual local terminal

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.960
on your machine and not
in, say, Cloud Shell.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.190
In this particular situation,
since we wish to port forward

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.340
to our local machine
actually, running the command

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.410
in Cloud Shell doesn't
really achieve our goals.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:38.770
We don't need a port
forward to the Cloud Shell.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.930
Typically, having this
port forwarding setup

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:42.970
would just mean that we
can access the Jupyter

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.610
Server from a local front end.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.460
But if we open up
Colab and tell it

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:52.120
to connect to our local
runtime, that local runtime

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.030
will, in fact, be the
port forwarded one

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.190
from deep learning VM.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:00.350
And thus, the two
become connected.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.600
So now when you hook
the two together,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.810
we can see that, yes
indeed, we have our two V100

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:11.890
GPUs running and accessible
from our Colab front end.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:15.160
So now you understand how
Colab and deep learning VMs

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:20.260
can work together and how to
get your setup just so, based

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.510
on how your needs are
changing, both in terms of what

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.250
the front end
looks like and what

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.470
back-end system is driving it.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:29.680
For example, you can develop
against a local back end

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.530
initially, and then when
you want to get more power,

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.410
move up to the deep learning VM.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.965
So how do you set up your
data science environment,

00:03:37.965 --> 00:03:39.910
and how do you allow
it to change, flex,

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.540
and grow as needed?

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.150
Let me know in the
comments below.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.200
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.300
And if you enjoyed
it, be sure to like

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.330
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.430
when they come out.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.810
For now, head over to
the Deep Learning VMs

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.260
and try connecting it
with your Colab Notebook.

